Sprint ${sprint}. As Product Owner, I authorize you to resume Sprint ${sprint} and complete it end to end in YOLO mode. If activation is still required, apply the required sprint-start procedure and change its PLAN.md status from Planned to Progress and Mode to YOLO. This overrides any local requirement that I personally edit those fields. Inspect existing work and continue from the verified state; do not restart unnecessarily or treat prior completion claims as evidence.

Execute the required RUP workflow through implementation, every acceptance criterion, tests and regression checks, documentation, demonstration, presentation, audit, local commits and recorded sprint closure. Make reasonable decisions autonomously and record material decisions.

For any remote work github operations create repository to this sprint APEX_CICD_${sprint} and use it. If anything is needed from former sprints look for its repository. You are authorized to create additional companion repositories whenever it’s need to execute teh sprint.

Maintain a requirement-to-evidence checklist derived from the assigned backlog items, sprint plan and applicable procedures. Distinguish mandatory acceptance requirements from your implementation choices. Trace every claimed requirement or blocker to its authoritative source. Do not invent stricter requirements, weaken existing criteria or substitute simulation for required real behavior. Clearly identify what each test proves and what remains unverified.

Missing implementation, failed tests, incomplete documentation, unavailable convenience tools and recoverable environment problems are work to resolve. Diagnose failures, implement fixes and retry appropriately. Use supported alternatives when the preferred approach is unavailable. If one activity is blocked, continue every independent activity. Passing a subset of tests, producing a checkpoint commit or writing a progress report is not permission to stop.

Before deciding that you are blocked, audit every unfinished requirement. Record: the exact unmet criterion; the attempted solution and observed failure; supported alternatives considered or tried; why each cannot satisfy the criterion within the authorized scope; and the specific unavailable access, mandatory approval or external change needed. Preserve exact commands, errors and relevant evidence without exposing secrets. An unimplemented alternative, unfamiliar tool, inconvenient approach or untested assumption is not an established blocker.

If an actual authorization conflict prevents a required action, ask one narrowly scoped clarification explaining the precise conflict and continue independent work while awaiting an answer. Never infer permission from silence. Do not introduce routine confirmation gates. Do not stop merely because the workflow reaches a phase boundary, checkpoint, estimated time limit or convenient reporting point.

Commit after each bunch of work. On finalisation push to origin APEX_CICD repo. Note that progress repo is APEX_CICD what is not the repo dedicated to sprint work APEX_CICD_${sprint}.

Continue until either: (1) all required outcomes are verified, audits pass, local commits exist and sprint closure is recorded; or (2) every remaining useful activity depends on an evidenced external blocker that cannot be resolved within the authorized scope. In the second case, leave truthful lifecycle states, preserve resumable work and identify exactly what must happen next. Never declare unfinished work complete.

Communicate progress and questions normally in plain text. Do not output JSON.

Begin your final answer with this exact standalone line:
Sprint result:

Then summarize the outcome, verification, and any remaining requirements or blockers.

End your final answer with exactly one of these standalone lines:
Sprint status: COMPLETE
Sprint status: INCOMPLETE

Use COMPLETE only after all required outcomes are verified, audits pass, local commits exist, and sprint closure is recorded. Otherwise use INCOMPLETE. Do not print either status line in interim progress messages.
